The potential vulnerability is related to HTTP Header Injection, also known as CRLF Injection. This vulnerability occurs because the function directly incorporates user-supplied input (`infoContent`) into an HTTP response header without proper sanitization or validation. If the `infoContent` contains carriage return (`\r`) and newline (`\n`) characters, an attacker can inject arbitrary new headers or manipulate existing ones, potentially leading to various attacks such as cross-site scripting (XSS), session fixation, or cache poisoning by altering the intended structure and content of the HTTP response.

